[Bo2's Example 3.11 (Tricky, as it comes)]

r2,0,b,2
r8,0,2,8
r3,2,a,3
r1,1,a,1
e,1,0,1
j,2,b,3*ir8

analysis: dc
note: Find the Norton equivalent between a and b — and watch it be refused. This one is meant to fail. The dependent source's value of 3 divides by zero; the next entry replaces it with a symbol.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/practice/bo2s-example-3-11-tricky-33.jpg [384px]
tool: th
n1: a
n2: b
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes


[Bo2's Example 3.11 (Tricky, with a symbol)]

r2,0,b,2
r8,0,2,8
r3,2,a,3
r1,1,a,1
e,1,0,1
j,2,b,x*ir8

analysis: dc
note: The same tricky circuit with x in place of 3 — now it solves, and the Evaluate line reads ino under the condition x = 3: 1 A. Ask Evaluate for req under the same condition and the answer is infinite: the denominator is zero at x = 3, which is the value the symbol replaced.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/practice/bo2s-example-3-11-tricky-33.jpg [384px]
tool: th
n1: a
n2: b
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes
evaluate: ino
evaluate_conditions: x = 3


[Bo2's Drill Exercise 3.13 (Tricky)]

ei,1,b,6
ed,b,0,2*x*ir2
r1,1,0,6
r2,0,a,2
jd,a,0,3*x*ir1

analysis: dc
note: Another tricky Norton equivalent: the dependent sources carry 2*x and 3*x in place of 2 and 3, and the Evaluate line reads ino under the condition x = 1: -3 A. Ask Evaluate for req under the same condition and the answer is infinite.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/practice/bo2s-drill-exercise-3-13-tricky-34.jpg [538px]
tool: th
n1: a
n2: b
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes
evaluate: ino
evaluate_conditions: x = 1


[TR5's Exercise 4-6 (Symbolic, input resistance)]

ei,3,0,vs
rf,3,2,rf
ro,2,t,ro
ed,2,0,μ*vrf

analysis: dc
note: First half of the symbolic exercise: find the input resistance seen by the source — rei, which comes back rf*(μ+1).
